1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,857
The Moon is running away from us.

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:05,026
Apollo astronauts left mirrors on the Moon.

3
00:00:05,026 --> 00:00:08,698
Observatories still fire lasers at them, and time the echo.

4
00:00:08,698 --> 00:00:11,498
About two and a half seconds, there and back.

5
00:00:11,983 --> 00:00:12,702
The result:

6
00:00:12,702 --> 00:00:16,752
the Moon drifts about 3.8 centimeters farther away every year.

7
00:00:16,752 --> 00:00:19,300
About as fast as your fingernails grow.

8
00:00:19,783 --> 00:00:22,140
Like and subscribe for more space what-ifs.

9
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:23,491
The cause is tides.

10
00:00:23,491 --> 00:00:26,367
They brake Earth's spin, and nudge the Moon outward.

11
00:00:26,883 --> 00:00:28,229
So our days get longer.

12
00:00:28,229 --> 00:00:32,968
Seventy million years ago, a day lasted only about twenty-three and a half hours.

13
00:00:33,483 --> 00:00:35,895
And in about six hundred million years,

14
00:00:35,895 --> 00:00:38,679
the Moon may look too small to cover the Sun.

15
00:00:38,679 --> 00:00:40,472
No more total solar eclipses.

16
00:00:40,983 --> 00:00:42,901
But what if the Moon vanished tonight?

17
00:00:42,901 --> 00:00:44,212
Full story on the channel.
